A||||9||RZURCTN||Oops!!!  Did I do that?!?||||5||
Z||000000||RZURCTN||01-06-2000||02:21 AM||||While I was reinstalling another engine, I screwed up and hooked up the battery cables backwards.  It fried the ECM and the alternator.  No problem, I had extras on hand.  Replaced them and got it running.  Now it idles rough and runs rough at higher rpms'.  It sounds like it's missing.  Could there be something else that I electrocuted when I hooked the cables up wrong?  Would a bad O2 sensor cause it to run really bad?  I'll set the timing tommorow, I don't think that it is a factor in haw badly it is running, because no matter where it is set, it runs like s*h*i*t!  Any insight fellas?  Thoughts and opinions are appreciated!!  Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||01-06-2000||04:44 AM||||When the engine is cold, the O2 sensor isn't used so it wouldn't matter if it was broken or still working. Unless ofcourse the coolant temperature sensor is shot too which causes the ECM to go into Closed Loop immediately. If you know of someone with a scantool that you can borrow, that would tell you a whole lot more.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BOILERMAKER||01-06-2000||09:02 AM||||Sounds about like what I am dealing with right now.  The only difference is that I haven't fried anything.  How are you setting the timing.  I know many threads in the past have been posted about this.  I will check in the manual tonight also.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RZURCTN||01-06-2000||10:41 AM||||I'll set the timing according to the Haynes manual and the Catalyst sticker in the trunk.  Set it to #1 and then check #4, get the average, if it matches, great!  If not, do it over!  For the 4 cyl. it's 8 degrees BTC.  I'll check that coolant sensor too.  Because if the O2 sensor isn't working, the ECM is supposed to set a default value and let it keep running. I wonder if it messed up the TPS?  I guess I can change it and find out!  I'll let you know what conspires!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||RZURCTN||01-06-2000||01:51 PM||||BINGO!!!!  It was the EGR valve!  Process of elimination led me to it!  I unplgged the vacuum line to it and everything settled right out.  Now I just need to pull it off and see if it needs to be cleaned or replaced.  ||||reg||3
Z||000005||DRH||01-06-2000||03:19 PM||||If unplugging the EGR valve solves the problem, chances are nothing is wrong with the valve. This probably means you are getting vacuum to the EGR when you shouldn't be. <P>I think you said this is a 4 cyl??? If so, most likely you have the EGR run to the wrong vacuum port on the TBI. The EGR runs off a port that has no vacuum at idle where most other vacuum ports have high vacuum at idle. Double check the vacuum line routing against the sticker inside the trunk.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||RZURCTN||01-06-2000||05:52 PM||||I'll have to check that out.  I'm pretty sure that I have everything hooked up right.  Thanks!||||reg||4
Z||000007||DRH||01-06-2000||06:03 PM||||I just remembered something... Did you put a jumper on the diagnostic terminal when you set the timing? I had a brain fart one time and set the timing without doing that. It ran rough... I unplugged the EGR... it ran better (have no idea why)... Still wasn't quite right... After I FINALLY reset the timing correctly, I plugged the EGR back up and everything was fine.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||RZURCTN||01-07-2000||12:52 AM||||I haven't had a chance to set the timing yet.  Would that really mess up the EGR valve?  I know about hooking up the diagnostic on the ALCL when setting the timing.  I just haven't got that far yet.  Anyways, I double-double checked the vacuum hose routing and it was correct.  I made sure that the hose wasn't blocked or restricted too.  So if it's only a matter of timing...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I should have it licked tomorrow!||||reg||1
Z||000009||BOILERMAKER||01-07-2000||08:45 AM||||Well, I unplugged my EGR last night and no difference.  Must be somewhere else.||||reg||1
